# Life Manager

A console-based personal life management system written in C++, combining **task management**, **expense tracking**, and **goal setting** into a single OOP-driven application.

## Overview

Life Manager lets each registered user manage three areas of their life from one account:

- **Tasks** — Academic and Daily tasks with descriptions, deadlines (date + time), categories, and status
- **Expenses** — Titled, categorized spending records with amounts, dates, and descriptions
- **Goals** — Personal goals with categories, deadlines, and a title/badge system that rewards consistent progress

The application is built around a small class hierarchy (polymorphic tasks, composed managers, and a `User` that owns them all) and runs entirely in the terminal — no external database or file storage is used.

## Features

### Accounts
- Registration with Full Name, unique Username, Date of Birth, and Password (with confirmation)
- Login via Username + Password
- Change password from within the app
- Profile view/update

### Task Manager
- Two task types: **Academic** (Exam, Assignment, Project, Midterm, Test, Submission, Viva, Presentation, Other) and **Daily** (Laundry, Cleaning, Exercise, Grocery Shopping, Cooking, Reading, Other)
- Each task stores a title, description, category, deadline **date and time**, and status
- Display all / completed / pending tasks (numbered lists)
- Search by ID, title, category, deadline, or status (title/category/status search is case-insensitive and partial-match)
- Mark complete, update, or delete — each shows the current task list first so you never need to remember an ID

### Expense Manager
- Category-first entry so titles describe the actual purchase (e.g. Category: `Food`, Title: `KFC Dinner`)
- Categories: Food, Transport, Education, Shopping, Bills, Entertainment, Health, Other
- Search by ID, title, category, or date
- Update any single field (title, amount, category, date, description) without retyping the rest
- Running total and highest-spending category shown on the dashboard

### Goal Manager
- Categories: Academic, Career, Health, Personal Development, Financial, Other
- Deadline tracking with automatic "Overdue" status once the deadline passes
- Title/badge system — unlocks a new title at 5, 10, 20, 30, 40, and 50 completed goals
- Search, update, complete, and delete follow the same guided flow as tasks

### Dashboard
Shown immediately after login:
- Pending, overdue, due-soon, and completed task counts
- Pending, overdue, and completed goal counts, plus progress toward the next title
- Total spent and highest-spending category
- Most recently earned title (if any)
- Full pending task list

## Design Principles

Every manager (Task, Expense, Goal) follows the same interaction pattern:

```
Display records → Select ID → Perform action → Confirmation message → Return to menu
```

- **Update** — shows all records, asks for an ID, then asks *which single field* to change
- **Delete** — shows all records, asks for an ID, then requires a Yes/No confirmation
- **Complete** — shows pending/incomplete records, asks for an ID, then marks it done
- **Search** — case-insensitive and substring-based across title, category, and status fields

## Architecture

```
Date, Time              Value classes for calendar dates and clock times
Task (abstract)         ├── AcademicTask
                         └── DailyTask
TaskManager              Owns a vector<Task*>; polymorphic dispatch via displayTask()
Expense / ExpenseManager Owns a vector<Expense*>
Goal / GoalManager       Owns a vector<Goal*>; tracks completed-goal badges/titles
User                      Composes TaskManager + ExpenseManager + GoalManager
Authentication            Verifies login and handles password changes (friend of User)
```

Coding conventions used throughout:
- No exception handling — invalid input is caught and re-prompted directly
- No file I/O — all data lives in memory for the session
- Auto-incrementing static IDs for tasks, expenses, goals, and users
- `ALL CAPS` prompts and messages
- `vector<T*>` for all collections, with manual `delete` in each manager's destructor

## Building

Requires a C++17-capable compiler (uses `<ctime>`, `<algorithm>`, `<cctype>`, `<utility>`).

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -o LifeManager LM7.cpp
./LifeManager
```

## Usage

1. Run the program and choose **Register** to create an account (Full Name, Username, DOB, Password).
2. Choose **Login** with your Username and Password.
3. From the dashboard, open the **User Menu** to reach the Task, Expense, or Goal Manager.
4. Add records, then use **Display / Search / Update / Delete / Complete** as needed — every destructive or ID-based action shows you the current list first.
5. **Logout** returns to the Register/Login screen; **Exit** closes the program.

## Notes

- All data is in-memory only; nothing is saved once the program exits.
- Usernames must be unique across all registered accounts; the app checks this at registration time.
- This is an academic OOP project (CS-116, Computer Systems Engineering) — the architecture intentionally favors clarity of design (inheritance, polymorphism, composition) over persistence or production-grade error handling.